# Line endings are load-bearing for a dotfiles installer.
#
#   1. szcdfi's own tests write and compare BYTES to prove its text handling
#      (UTF-8 without BOM, destination-EOL preservation). The fixture sources
#      under tests/fixtures/ must therefore check out with stable, known
#      endings on every machine, or a byte comparison would differ by platform.
#   2. The fixture shell scripts (bin/*.sh) are POSIX scripts; a CRLF checkout
#      would put a trailing \r on their shebang line.
#   3. A .cmd / .bat launcher wants the opposite: older cmd.exe mis-parses
#      LF-only batch files.
#
# So: LF everywhere, CRLF only for batch launchers. This governs what git
# checks out for THIS repo's files; it does not change how the installer treats
# a user's destination files at run time -- that is decided by the engine, whose
# tests write bytes rather than relying on this.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
